[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) merges 412 reports under one story: the parser reads dates month
first, day-first tenants get the day and month flipped. `causes.ts` already knows that story
has two different bugs inside it — `parser` and `hint`. It does not know about a third one.

<Callout kind="warn">
Decision 0001 merges on subject line alone, never the body. `causeOf()` only ever asks a
report's tenant and channel, never its body either. Nothing in the pipeline ever reads what a
report actually says — so nothing in the pipeline could have found this on its own.
</Callout>

## What was actually merged

<SplitTable ticket={4471} />

`parser` and `hint` are the two bugs the desk already modeled, counted exactly as the desk's own
export counts them — this page changes no counting logic, only adds a third row. `timezone` is
not a day/month mix-up at all: it's 5 reports where the invite's calendar day depends on which
timezone opened it, or showed up a day off with no month confusion in sight. `desk-notes.json`
already named these 5 ids under `oneDayMove` — the desk noticed, and still filed every one of
them under `parser` too, footnoted, because `causeOf()` has no third bucket to put them in. So
`parser`'s 379 and `timezone`'s 5 overlap: same 412 reports, read a second way, not a different
split of them.

## Why these 5 aren't `parser` or `hint`

A `parser`/`hint` report reads as a day↔month swap: *"Our 7 April reminder went out on 4
July"* is 7/4 read as 4/7. None of these do that:

- **R-41202** — *"Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11 May."* Same month, one day
  off. Not a swap.
- **R-41209** — *"Every booking in June is one day off. Only June."* Month-specific, not a
  day/month confusion.
- **R-41236** — *"The invite arrived before I made the booking."* No date-order bug produces this
  at all.
- **R-41344** — *"Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting."*
  Two recipients, two timezones, two calendar days for one instant.
- **R-41620** — *"Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday."* One day off, one month, no swap.

Scanning all 412 bodies for the swap pattern (`D Month` / `D Month`, values exchanged) matches
403 of them. These 5 are the only ones that don't, and they're exactly the 5 `desk-notes.json`
already flagged — independent confirmation, not a guess.

## Tickets filed

One GitHub issue per real problem, each carrying its report count. `#4471` itself closed 05 Oct
with an engineering update covering two of the three — the third was never part of that update,
because the desk's own classifier never separated it out:

- [`parser`](https://github.com/lorimccurry/what-it-shows/issues/1) — 379 reports — **fixed**:
  `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`
- [`hint`](https://github.com/lorimccurry/what-it-shows/issues/2) — 33 reports — **won't fix**:
  booking-v3 form retired
- [`timezone`](https://github.com/lorimccurry/what-it-shows/issues/3) — 5 reports — **open**:
  not part of the 05 Oct update, still unaddressed

